# Multi-block SACK driving multiple selective retransmits.
#
# Setup: we send 5 MSS-payload segments. The peer SACKs segments #2 and
# #4 (so #1, #3, and #5 are the gaps from the sender's perspective).
# Under RFC 6675 with IsLost = (≥ 3 MSS sacked above seq), we should
# retransmit segments at the gaps that satisfy IsLost.
#
# With wscale=0 and TS-effective MSS = 1448:
#   seg #1 = [1, 1449)
#   seg #2 = [1449, 2897)   ← SACKed
#   seg #3 = [2897, 4345)
#   seg #4 = [4345, 5793)   ← SACKed
#   seg #5 = [5793, 7241)
#
# Only 2 segments are SACKed (fewer than DupThresh), so RACK (RFC 8985)
# waits one reordering window (min_RTT/4 = 1 ms here) before declaring
# loss. Then every unacknowledged segment sent before the most recently
# SACKed one (#4) is lost: #1 and #3. #5 was sent after #4 and is not.

--connect 10.0.0.1:49152 10.0.0.2:80
+0 > SEW 0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460, wscale 5, sackOK, TS val * ecr 0>
+.005 < S. 0:0(0) ack 1 win 32768 <mss 1460, wscale 0, sackOK, TS val 1 ecr *>
+0 > . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 1>

# Push 5 segments worth of data (5 * 1448 = 7240). IW=10 lets us emit
# all in one burst.
--send 7240
+0 > P. 1:1449(1448) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 1>
+0 > P. 1449:2897(1448) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 1>
+0 > P. 2897:4345(1448) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 1>
+0 > P. 4345:5793(1448) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 1>
+0 > P. 5793:7241(1448) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 1>

# Peer SACKs #2 and #4 in a single ACK with two blocks.
+.005 < . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 32768 <sack 1449:2897 4345:5793, TS val 2 ecr *>

# PRR (RFC 6937) clocks recovery by delivered data; the timer-driven entry
# is credited one segment, so the lowest hole goes first (as Linux does).
+.002 > P. 1:1449(1448) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 2>

# Its ACK covers #1 and #2 and still SACKs #4: the next hole follows.
+.010 < . 1:1(0) ack 2897 win 32768 <sack 4345:5793, TS val 3 ecr *>
+0 > P. 2897:4345(1448) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 3>
